1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:05,403
Say a report lists an amino-chain, a peptide, as 98 percent pure.

2
00:00:05,403 --> 00:00:06,731
What does that number mean?

3
00:00:06,731 --> 00:00:10,455
A lab separates the sample, and a detector draws peaks.

4
00:00:10,455 --> 00:00:12,760
One tall peak is the target chain.

5
00:00:12,760 --> 00:00:15,169
The small ones are other things that came along.

6
00:00:15,169 --> 00:00:20,052
Purity compares the area of the tall peak to the area of all the peaks.

7
00:00:20,052 --> 00:00:22,513
But a peak is a signal, not a weight.

8
00:00:22,513 --> 00:00:25,182
Different pieces absorb light differently.

9
00:00:25,182 --> 00:00:32,317
In one lab study, tryptophan absorbed about thirty times more than a plain peptide bond at a common wavelength.

10
00:00:32,317 --> 00:00:34,153
So area is not mass.

11
00:00:34,153 --> 00:00:36,692
Purity says how clean the signal looks.

12
00:00:36,692 --> 00:00:39,283
It does not say how much of the vial is chain.

13
00:00:39,283 --> 00:00:41,067
Those are two different questions.

14
00:00:41,067 --> 00:00:42,994
Follow for more peptide math.

